{{unsolved|computer science|Do one-way functions exist?}}

In [[computer science]], a '''one-way function''' is a [[function (mathematics)|function]] that is easy to compute on every input, but hard to [[Inverse function|invert]] given the [[image (mathematics)|image]] of a random input.  Here, "easy" and "hard" are to be understood in the sense of [[computational complexity theory]], specifically the theory of [[polynomial time]] problems. This has nothing to do with whether the function is [[One-to-one function|one-to-one]]; finding any one input with the desired image is considered a successful inversion.  (See {{section link||Theoretical definition}}, below.)

The existence of such one-way functions is still an open [[conjecture]]. Their existence would prove that the [[complexity classes]] [[P = NP problem|P and NP are not equal]], thus resolving the foremost unsolved question of theoretical computer science.<ref name=Goldreich>[[Oded Goldreich]] (2001). Foundations of Cryptography: Volume 1, Basic Tools ([http://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~oded/PSBookFrag/part2N.ps draft available] from author's site). Cambridge University Press. {{isbn|0-521-79172-3}}. See also [http://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~oded/foc-book.html wisdom.weizmann.ac.il].</ref>{{rp|ex. 2.2, page 70}} The converse is not known to be tru

